  "worked_solution": "The mass of the plate occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\u222c}_{D}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. \n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & =e \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the $x$-limits are $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/95a6d9b0bc464c719311dc900967ebd06a3aa3d3004cfe5303359eecb20551e7.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{e}^{x}\\le y\\le e}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y]_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061e-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the iron plate is $M=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
